\description{
This function reads morphological data from an Excel spreadsheet,
generates standardized species descriptions in plain text and Word document formats,
and exports them to a Word (.docx) file. It handles complex morphological data
structures including measurements, subterms and grouping of related characters.
The function automatically detects measurement ranges and formats them with
en dashes (e.g., "4.3–14.5 cm"). Optionally, it can compute and print averages
for user-selected measurement columns when a species has more than a threshold
number of values (see \code{avg_cols} and \code{avg_min_n}).
Rare small and large values are written between parentheses, as usual in
taxonomic descriptions, e.g. "(2–)6–40(–55) m" (see \code{extremes}).
}
\details{
\strong{Measurements}

Each cell of a measurement column has the measurements of one specimen. When a
specimen was measured more than once (e.g. several leaves), the values are
separated by a vertical bar, e.g. \code{"4.5 | 7 | 11 | 14"}. The values of all
specimens of a species are pooled to build its range.

Rare values are written between parentheses in two ways:
\describe{
\item{\strong{Marked in the spreadsheet}}{Values written as in a description,
e.g. \code{"(5–)7–38(–45)"} or \code{"(2–)6 | 9 | 12"}, are always kept as
extremes when they are beyond the range of the other values.}
\item{\strong{Automatic}}{With \code{extremes = c(0.1, 0.9)}, when a species has
at least \code{extremes_min_n} values, the range covers the values from the
10th to the 90th percentile, and the smallest and largest values outside it
are written between parentheses. The range limits are always measured values.
With fewer values, the range goes from the smallest to the largest value.}
}

\strong{Counts}

Columns with just numbers, e.g. \code{LEAF SECONDARY-VENATION pairs} with
\code{"6 | 8 | 12"}, or \code{FLOWER STAMEN number} with \code{"10"}, are counts:
their values are pooled into ranges, with rare values between parentheses, as
the measurements. The lowercase words at the end of the column name are written
after the range, e.g. "6–12 pairs", or "fascicles 5–15 per axis" for
\code{STAMINATE-INFLORESCENCE FASCICLES per axis}, except "number" and
"count", e.g. "stamen 8–10". Write counts in
their own columns, and any text in another column, e.g. "curved distally
towards the marginal vein" in \code{LEAF SECONDARY-VENATION}.

\strong{Maximum values, thickness and diameter}

Measurements known only by their maximum are written as \code{"up to 5"} (or
\code{"to 5"}) in all specimens of a species, and become e.g. "axis up to 5 cm
long". Columns of thickness and diameter, e.g. \code{FRUIT PERICARP thickness (mm)}
and \code{INFLORESCENCE CAPITULUM diameter (mm)}, are written as "1–2 mm thick" and
"3–4 mm in diam.".

\strong{Punctuation}

Each organ (e.g. \code{STIPULE}) is a sentence. Its single traits are separated
by commas, while parts with their own traits in lowercase (e.g.
\code{HABIT STEM color}, \code{LEAF PETIOLE shape}) start after a semicolon, e.g.
"Shrub 1.8 m tall, erect, branched; stem striated, gray, glabrous. Stipule 1 pair,
lateral to petiole, 11–16 × 2–3 mm, lanceolate, base truncate, apex acute.". A part
without traits in lowercase, like \code{STIPULE BASE}, is a single trait.

\strong{Column naming conventions}

The function relies on a structured column naming system to parse and organize
morphological traits. Columns must follow these conventions:

\describe{
\item{\strong{Main character groups}}{Use ALL CAPS for the primary category:
\code{HABIT}, \code{STIPULE}, \code{LEAF}, \code{INFLORESCENCE},
\code{FLOWER}, \code{FRUIT}, \code{SEED}. These will become section headers. Organs of two words are joined by a hyphen,
e.g. \code{LEAF-BLADE} and \code{STAMINATE-INFLORESCENCE} for "Leaf blade" and
"Staminate inflorescence", as are terms like \code{SECONDARY-VEINS}.}

\item{\strong{Simple characters}}{Lowercase descriptive terms after the main group.
Examples: \code{LEAF shape}, \code{LEAF margin}, \code{FLOWER symmetry}.
These will be appended directly after the lowercase group name.}

\item{\strong{Hierarchical characters}}{Use ALL CAPS for subgroup terms.
Examples: \code{LEAF PETIOLE length (cm)}, \code{FLOWER SEPALS shape}.
The capitalized term (\code{PETIOLE}, \code{SEPALS}) is extracted once
and used as a subheading, followed by its traits in the order of the columns,
e.g. "stamen 10, sessile, anthers brownish, glabrous, 5–6 × 1 mm".}

\item{\strong{Measurements}}{Include units in parentheses for automatic detection:
\code{LEAF length (cm)}, \code{STIPULE width (mm)}, \code{HABIT height (m)}.
Multiple measurements (e.g., length + width) are automatically combined
with "×" and the unit is placed at the end.}

\item{\strong{Column name flexibility}}{Spaces, dots, or underscores can be used
as separators. All of the following are valid:
\itemize{
\item \code{STIPULE length (mm)}
\item \code{STIPULE.length.(mm)}
\item \code{STIPULE_length_mm}
\item \code{LEAF PETIOLE shape}
\item \code{LEAF.PETIOLE.shape}
}}
}

\strong{Example dataset}

The package includes an example dataset \code{chars_dataset} containing
morphological data for 15 species of \emph{Ouratea} (Ochnaceae) compiled from
herbarium specimens and field observations for a taxonomic revision. This dataset
demonstrates proper column naming conventions and can be used to test the function:

\preformatted{
data(chars_dataset)
head(names(chars_dataset))
}

\strong{Creating a template}

To create a properly formatted Excel template with all required columns for your
plant group, use the companion function \code{\link{barroso_chars_template}}.
This function generates a ready-to-use template based on your species list and
selected plant group (e.g., "Ochnaceae", "Asteraceae", "Orchidaceae",
"Leguminosae-Papilionoideae"), saving hours of manual column creation.

\preformatted{
# Create a template for Ochnaceae
barroso_chars_template(
  species_df = my_species_list,
  plant_group = "Ochnaceae",
  filename = "Ochnaceae_template"
)
}
}
